{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790439692,"nonce":"nh/uGYkITd+qb6wRQyzirEdmGDk=","title":"LICM hoists a load out of the branch that guards its pointer","message":"HIR LICM moves a Load whose pointer is loop-invariant into the\npreheader even when the Load sits behind a branch inside the loop. A\nload is not free to speculate: the branch may be what makes the pointer\nvalid. A set of frozensets probed in a loop crashed this way: the\ninlined equality of two int sets reads the index through the control\nentry's hash word only when that word is nonzero, and LICM hoisted the\nload of the probe key's index to the function entry, where a small key\nset (no index, word zero) made it a null dereference.\n\n    big = set()\n    for i in range(40):\n        big.add(frozenset([i, i + 1]))\n    print(len(big))\n\nsegfaulted in zb_set_find_hashed_\u003cs\u003e under ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1\n(the call compiled lazily and inlined). The fix hoists a Load only\nfrom a block that dominates every latch of the loop; pure non-trapping\ninstructions still hoist from anywhere.","files":null}]}